{"claims":[{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven in sociale en voedselrelaties","claim":"Yellowstonewolves participate in social relationships and food relationships.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Wolves are highly social animals and live in packs.","narrative":"Yellowstone’s wolves are portrayed not as solitary ecosystem controllers but as social predators whose kills feed other animals","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The title concisely frames the source’s descriptions of wolf social life and the food provided by wolf kills.","materiality":"Frames the piece’s central idea."},{"excerpt":"Yellowstonewolven leven in sociale familiegroepen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves live in social family groups.","category":"entity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family","narrative":"Introduce Yellowstonewolven als sociale familiegroepen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This matches the source’s description of packs as social families.","materiality":"Establishes the wolves’ social context."},{"excerpt":"Ze jagen samen","claim":"Yellowstone wolves hunt together.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"group hunting of large prey","narrative":"met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source identifies group hunting as a feature of wolves’ social living.","materiality":"A concrete example of social behavior."},{"excerpt":"zorgen voor hun jongen.","claim":"Yellowstone wolves cooperate in caring for their young.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"cooperative care of offspring","narrative":"met onder meer gezamenlijke jacht en zorg voor jongen.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This accurately renders the source’s account of cooperative offspring care.","materiality":"A concrete example of social behavior."},{"excerpt":"Wanneer wolven een prooi doden, wordt die ook voedsel voor andere dieren: van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","claim":"Animals including ravens, coyotes, bears, and invertebrates feed on the remains of wolf kills.","category":"other","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Many other animals benefit from wolf kills. For example, when wolves kill an elk, ravens and magpies arrive almost immediately. Coyotes arrive soon after, waiting nearby until the wolves are sated. Bears will attempt to chase the wolves away, and are usually successful. Many other animals—from eagles to invertebrates—consume the remains.","narrative":"een wolvendoding voedsel biedt aan dieren van raven en coyotes tot beren en ongewervelden.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The listed animals are supported by the source. It gives an elk kill as an example; the piece’s general wording is consistent with the broader statement that many animals benefit from wolf kills.","materiality":"Shows the food relationships associated with wolf kills."},{"excerpt":"De prooien waarop wolven jagen, veranderen eveneens.","claim":"The prey composition of Yellowstone wolves changes over time.","category":"chronology","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in Their Prey\n\nFrom 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"De veranderde samenstelling van hun prooi in verschillende perioden maakt concreet dat predatiepatronen veranderen","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source documents a change in the composition of wolf prey across periods.","materiality":"Introduces the example of changing prey composition."},{"excerpt":"Onder de vroege-winterprooien verschilde de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007.","claim":"The proportion of elk calves to adult male elk among early-winter wolf prey differed between 1995–2000 and 2001–2007.","category":"quantity","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"From 1995 to 2000, in early winter, elk calves comprised 50% of wolf prey, and bull elk comprised 25%. That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007","narrative":"de verschuiving in de verhouding tussen elandkalveren en volwassen mannelijke elanden onder de vroege-winterprooien in 1995–2000 en 2001–2007","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source specifies that the ratio reversed. The piece only says it differed, which is accurate though less specific.","materiality":"A concrete, time-bounded example; the omitted reversal does not make the statement misleading."},{"excerpt":"Die verschuiving hangt samen met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","claim":"The prey-composition shift is associated with changes in prey availability and vulnerability.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"That ratio reversed from 2001 to 2007, indicating changes in prey vulnerability and availability.","narrative":"de bron verbindt die veranderingen met de beschikbaarheid en kwetsbaarheid van prooien.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source says the reversed ratio indicates changes in vulnerability and availability; the piece does not attribute the shift to one specific cause.","materiality":"Qualifies how the shift should be interpreted without asserting a single cause."},{"excerpt":"Veranderingen in predatie en de effecten daarvan op elanden vallen samen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, het beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","claim":"Changes in wolf predation and its effects on elk occur in interaction with other factors, including other predators, ungulate management outside the park, and weather.","category":"cause","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Changes in wolf predation patterns and impacts on prey species like elk are inextricably linked to other factors, such as other predators, management of ungulates outside the park, and weather (e.g. drought, winter severity).","narrative":"veranderingen in predatie en de effecten op elanden samenvallen met andere factoren, waaronder andere roofdieren, beheer van hoefdieren buiten het park en het weer.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source names these same factors and describes their relationship with predation patterns and elk impacts as interconnected. The piece does not claim their relative contributions are known.","materiality":"Preserves the source’s multi-factor account rather than assigning a single cause."},{"excerpt":"De wolven maken deel uit van een samenspel van sociale en voedselrelaties","claim":"Wolves are part of an interacting network of social and food relationships.","category":"interpretation","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"The pack is a complex social family, with older members (often the alpha male and alpha female) and subordinates, each having individual personality traits and roles within the pack. Research in Yellowstone since reintroduction has highlighted the adaptive value of social living in wolves – from cooperative care of offspring, group hunting of large prey, defense of territory and prey carcasses","narrative":"wolven maken deel uit van sociale en voedselrelaties","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"This summarizes the source’s descriptions of pack social life and food relationships around wolf kills and prey.","materiality":"Connects the piece’s social and food-related observations."},{"excerpt":"ze zijn niet de enige bepalende kracht.","claim":"Wolves are not the sole factor determining predation patterns or elk outcomes.","category":"scope","label":"F0 faithful","evidence":"Consequently, changes in prey selection and kill rates through time result from complex interactions among these factors.","narrative":"niet bij wolven als enige bepalende kracht.","narrativeRelation":"supports","origin":"propagated","explanation":"The source attributes changing prey selection and kill rates to complex interactions among multiple factors. This does not claim that wolves have no effect, or that plant effects or an ecosystem-wide cascade were measured.","materiality":"Prevents an unsupported single-cause or ecosystem-controller interpretation."}]}

